  <Scene>
    <WorldInfo title="Different Motions, Identical Samples" info="&quot;X3D Math Lab • Static sampled geometry and authored lesson parameters; not a proof, live controller, or checkpoint.&quot;">
      <MetadataString containerField="metadata" name="mathlab.sample" reference="mathlab.x3d/1" value="&quot;{\&quot;adapter\&quot;:\&quot;mathlab.x3d/1\&quot;,\&quot;axes\&quot;:\&quot;Horizontal: time 0–2 s (display x=3t−3). Vertical: signal amplitude. Amber dots are samples at j/fₛ; all lines use the same amplitude scale.\&quot;,\&quot;bounds\&quot;:{\&quot;center\&quot;:[0.0,0.0,0.037500000000000006],\&quot;size\&quot;:[6.721,2.9,0.6950000000000001]},\&quot;configuration_sha256\&quot;:\&quot;5e993013dd7146c961356b411655d3b686877b5e262c819d1a0a52b8262b12bf\&quot;,\&quot;insights\&quot;:[\&quot;The curves represent sample-equivalent candidates, not a reconstruction and its error. They can coincide for some controls, including an original frequency below Nyquist; the default reveals distinct between-sample motion. Amber samples alone cannot choose between unrestricted candidates.\&quot;,\&quot;At t=j/fₛ their phase difference is an integer multiple of 2π. Frequencies f−kfₛ give an infinite family of sample-equivalent complex phase advances.\&quot;,\&quot;The displayed real cosine also has a sign/phase symmetry: a negative signed frequency with phase φ equals a positive frequency with phase −φ. Signed alias is a convenient phase convention.\&quot;,\&quot;The classical ideal sampling theorem assumes a known strict bandlimit and an ideal sampling/reconstruction setting. This finite two-second display proves neither arbitrary reconstruction nor frequency uniqueness.\&quot;],\&quot;lesson\&quot;:\&quot;aliasing\&quot;,\&quot;metrics\&quot;:[{\&quot;id\&quot;:\&quot;alias\&quot;,\&quot;label\&quot;:\&quot;Signed alias frequency\&quot;,\&quot;note\&quot;:\&quot;Representative in [−fₛ/2,fₛ/2); negative means reversed phase advance, not a negative magnitude.\&quot;,\&quot;unit\&quot;:\&quot;Hz\&quot;,\&quot;value\&quot;:-1},{\&quot;id\&quot;:\&quot;nyquist\&quot;,\&quot;label\&quot;:\&quot;Nyquist frequency fₛ/2\&quot;,\&quot;note\&quot;:\&quot;\&quot;,\&quot;unit\&quot;:\&quot;Hz\&quot;,\&quot;value\&quot;:4.0},{\&quot;id\&quot;:\&quot;samples\&quot;,\&quot;label\&quot;:\&quot;Shared samples in two seconds\&quot;,\&quot;note\&quot;:\&quot;\&quot;,\&quot;unit\&quot;:\&quot;\&quot;,\&quot;value\&quot;:17},{\&quot;id\&quot;:\&quot;sample_error\&quot;,\&quot;label\&quot;:\&quot;Maximum sample disagreement\&quot;,\&quot;note\&quot;:\&quot;Floating-point diagnostic; the sampled values agree algebraically.\&quot;,\&quot;unit\&quot;:\&quot;\&quot;,\&quot;value\&quot;:6.106226635438361e-15},{\&quot;id\&quot;:\&quot;removed\&quot;,\&quot;label\&quot;:\&quot;Whole turns removed per sample\&quot;,\&quot;note\&quot;:\&quot;\&quot;,\&quot;unit\&quot;:\&quot;\&quot;,\&quot;value\&quot;:1}],\&quot;parameters\&quot;:{\&quot;frequency\&quot;:7,\&quot;phase\&quot;:0.65,\&quot;sample_rate\&quot;:8},\&quot;sample_schema\&quot;:\&quot;mathlab.sample/1\&quot;,\&quot;sample_sha256\&quot;:\&quot;64329305647eb230290cc5ce606e99277a0d5b8ab5978adf26b1badc93bd1681\&quot;,\&quot;scope\&quot;:\&quot;Static sampled geometry and authored lesson parameters; not a proof, live controller, or checkpoint.\&quot;,\&quot;title\&quot;:\&quot;Different Motions, Identical Samples\&quot;,\&quot;view\&quot;:\&quot;planar\&quot;}&quot;" />
    </WorldInfo>
